<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Aranjuez (1801)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1801</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Aranjuez of 21 March 1801 was an agreement between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> confirming the secret Treaty of San Ildefonso of 1800. Duke Ferdinand of Parma renounced his duchy, and his son Louis received Tuscany as the new Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a>, in return for Spain's retrocession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a> to France.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-aranjuez-1801.jpg" alt="Treaty of Aranjuez (1801)" width="320" height="384" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Enok · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Italia_1796-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
